//! image/width_profile.rye -- the WIDTH PROFILE of a point set: the full per-orientation width(theta) anisotropy signature, read as
//! the shape's PERPENDICULAR extent flush with EVERY convex-hull edge (not only the narrowest), the minimum of which is exactly
//! image/min_width.rye's breadth, chosen by the SAME EXACT integer comparison and NO tolerance -- the anisotropy fingerprint the
//! single min-width number is blind to.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- image/min_width.rye reads the ONE narrowest slot a shape passes edge-first, the minimum over
//! the convex hull's edges of the perpendicular extent. That minimum is a single fact: how THIN the shape is at its thinnest. It
//! cannot tell a 2x2 square from a 2x8 bar, for both are exactly 2 thin. This rung reads the WHOLE function instead of its floor:
//! one width sample per hull edge, in hull order, so the shape's thickness as it TURNS becomes visible. The square reads the same
//! width from every edge -- isotropic, as wide as it is thin. The bar reads 2 across its length and 8 across its thickness -- an
//! anisotropy of four. Two shapes one breadth cannot separate, the profile tells apart at a glance.
//!
//! The SAME ROTATING-CALIPERS construction image/min_width.rye rides, kept whole: for each hull edge measure the shape's
//! PERPENDICULAR extent -- the range of the cross product of the edge vector against every vertex -- and the width along that edge is
//! `cross_range_e / sqrt(edge_len_sq_e)`, so its SQUARE is the exact rational `cross_range_e^2 / edge_len_sq_e`. This rung keeps
//! every sample rather than only the minimum, then selects the min AND the max edge-normal width by the SAME EXACT i128
//! cross-multiplication (`cross_i^2 * D_j` against `cross_j^2 * D_i`, the square roots cleared by squaring) -- no tolerance ever
//! picks the wrong extreme. Every DECISION is integer arithmetic; only the reported `width`, `angle`, and the `anisotropy` ratio,
//! irrational because they divide by a square root, are read in f64, exactly as image/min_width.rye reports its own width.
//!
//! The DECISIVE facts:
//!
//!   THE PROFILE SEES ELONGATION THE BREADTH IS BLIND TO -- a 2x2 square and a 2x8 bar share a minimum width of EXACTLY 2 (proven
//!   against the real image/min_width.rye), yet the square's every edge-normal width is 2 (ISOTROPIC, anisotropy 1) while the bar's
//!   run 2 and 8 (anisotropy EXACTLY 4). One number cannot separate them; the whole profile does.
//!
//!   THE MINIMUM SAMPLE IS THE BREADTH -- the smallest width in the profile is byte-for-byte image/min_width.rye's result (same exact
//!   rational), because the minimum width of a convex polygon is always realized flush with a hull edge (a theorem the caliper sweep
//!   rests on). This rung's floor and image/min_width.rye agree by construction, not by rounding.
//!
//!   THE MAXIMUM SAMPLE IS EDGE-FLUSH, NOT THE DIAMETER -- the widest edge-normal width is the maximum over the hull's OWN edge
//!   orientations, a distinct fact from the true diameter (image/convex_diameter.rye), whose antipodal span need not be edge-aligned.
//!   For the 2x8 bar the widest edge-normal width is EXACTLY 8, STRICTLY below the diameter sqrt(68) ~ 8.25 -- the profile samples
//!   edge directions, never the farthest vertex pair.
//!
//! `is_isotropic(profile)` decides whether a shape is as wide as it is thin in every edge direction (max width equals min width
//! EXACTLY, by `max_num * min_den == min_num * max_den`), never a float compare; `at_least_as_elongated(profile, ratio)` decides
//! whether the widest edge-normal width is at least `ratio` times the breadth by the exact rule `max_num * min_den >= ratio^2 *
//! min_num * max_den` -- the honest anisotropy dual of image/min_width.rye's `passes_slot`.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the per-edge caliper sweep, the min/max selection, and the reads over the profile -- no
//! gather, no blend, no codec, no color, no pixel-writing loop. image/convex_hull.rye owns the hull and the Point type;
//! image/min_width.rye owns the single breadth this profile's floor agrees with; image/convex_diameter.rye owns the antipodal span
//! this profile's ceiling is honestly distinct from. The width profile of a boundary ring is the width profile of its whole shape --
//! interior pixels lie inside the hull, never needed.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: the profile holds one sample per hull edge, a hull of at most `convex_hull_max_points`
//! vertices (a boundary perimeter far under 2^13), and a longer input refuses through the hull's own `HullTooLong`; an empty input
//! refuses `EmptySource`; a hull of fewer than three vertices (a point or a segment) has no slot to measure and refuses
//! `DegenerateHull` by name. Coordinates stay under `width_profile_max_coord` (2^14), so a cross product stays far under 2^30 in i64,
//! its square under 2^60, and the i128 comparison has room to spare -- no accumulator wraps. All-or-nothing, never a half-built
//! profile. No network, no key, no funds -- the anisotropy signature a surface names the day it asks not how thin a shape is, but how
//! its thinness turns, on our own open geometry.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/width_profile.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const contour = @import("contour.rye");
const convex_hull = @import("convex_hull.rye");
const min_width = @import("min_width.rye");
const convex_diameter = @import("convex_diameter.rye");
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");

pub const width_profile_version = "20260818.210717";

/// Every hull coordinate stays under this bound, so a cross product stays under 2^30 in i64, its square under 2^60, and the i128
/// anisotropy cross-multiplication far under its ceiling. The same order of magnitude image/min_width.rye keeps for its own read.
pub const width_profile_max_coord: u32 = min_width.min_width_max_coord;

/// The most a caller may ask of `at_least_as_elongated` -- a ratio in the coordinate range, so `ratio^2 * min_num * max_den` stays
/// far under the i128 ceiling.
pub const width_profile_max_ratio: u32 = 1 << 14;

pub const WidthProfileError = error{
    DegenerateHull,
};

/// One WIDTH SAMPLE -- the shape's perpendicular extent flush with a single hull edge. The exact squared width is the rational
/// `width_sq_num / width_sq_den` (the denominator the supporting edge's length squared, the numerator the perpendicular cross range
/// squared); `width` and `angle` are read in f64 because the width divides by a square root. `support_i` and `support_j` name the two
/// hull vertices whose edge this width is flush with; the width itself runs PERPENDICULAR to that edge.
pub const WidthSample = struct {
    support_i: u32,
    support_j: u32,
    width_sq_num: u64,
    width_sq_den: u64,
    width: f64,
    angle: f64,
};

/// The WIDTH PROFILE -- one `WidthSample` per hull edge in hull order, and the indices of the narrowest (`min_index`, the breadth =
/// image/min_width.rye) and the widest edge-normal (`max_index`) samples, both selected by exact i128 comparison.
pub const WidthProfile = struct {
    samples: []WidthSample,
    min_index: u32,
    max_index: u32,
};

/// The squared length of the edge a->b, exact in u64. Distinct hull vertices give a strictly positive value, a safe denominator.
/// Coordinates under `width_profile_max_coord` keep each squared difference under 2^28.
fn edge_len_sq(a: contour.Point, b: contour.Point) u64 {
    const dx: i64 = @as(i64, b.x) - @as(i64, a.x);
    const dy: i64 = @as(i64, b.y) - @as(i64, a.y);
    const s: i64 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    // invariant: two in-image points give a squared length far under the i64 ceiling.
    assert(s >= 0);
    assert(s < (@as(i64, 1) << 30));
    return @intCast(s);
}

/// TRUE when squared width `na/da` is STRICTLY smaller than `nb/db`, decided by an exact i128 cross-multiplication (`na * db <
/// nb * da`). Because width is non-negative, comparing the squares compares the widths; positive edge-length denominators preserve
/// the inequality's direction. The same exact rule image/min_width.rye selects its floor by.
fn width_less(na: u64, da: u64, nb: u64, db: u64) bool {
    // invariant: a width denominator is a squared edge length of distinct vertices, strictly positive.
    assert(da >= 1);
    assert(db >= 1);
    const lhs: i128 = @as(i128, na) * @as(i128, db);
    const rhs: i128 = @as(i128, nb) * @as(i128, da);
    return lhs < rhs;
}

/// TRUE when squared width `na/da` is STRICTLY greater than `nb/db`, the exact mirror of `width_less` for selecting the widest
/// edge-normal sample.
fn width_greater(na: u64, da: u64, nb: u64, db: u64) bool {
    // invariant: a width denominator is a squared edge length of distinct vertices, strictly positive.
    assert(da >= 1);
    assert(db >= 1);
    const lhs: i128 = @as(i128, na) * @as(i128, db);
    const rhs: i128 = @as(i128, nb) * @as(i128, da);
    return lhs > rhs;
}

/// The WIDTH PROFILE of any point set -- build the convex hull, then for each hull edge measure the shape's perpendicular
/// cross-product extent over that edge, keeping every sample and tracking the exact minimum and maximum. Refuses `EmptySource` /
/// `HullTooLong` through the hull, and `DegenerateHull` when the hull is a single point or segment (no width to measure).
pub fn width_profile(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, points: []const contour.Point) !WidthProfile {
    const hull = try convex_hull.convex_hull(allocator, points);
    // invariant: a width profile needs a real hull -- at least a triangle.
    if (hull.len < 3) return error.DegenerateHull;
    // invariant: the hull never exceeds the point ceiling, so the sample buffer and every accumulator below stay bounded.
    assert(hull.len <= convex_hull.convex_hull_max_points);

    const samples = try allocator.alloc(WidthSample, hull.len);
    var min_index: usize = 0;
    var max_index: usize = 0;

    var e: usize = 0;
    while (e < hull.len) : (e += 1) {
        const a = hull[e];
        const b = hull[(e + 1) % hull.len];
        const ex: i64 = @as(i64, b.x) - @as(i64, a.x);
        const ey: i64 = @as(i64, b.y) - @as(i64, a.y);
        // invariant: consecutive hull vertices are distinct, so the supporting edge has real length.
        assert(ex != 0 or ey != 0);

        var min_cross: i64 = 0;
        var max_cross: i64 = 0;
        var v: usize = 0;
        while (v < hull.len) : (v += 1) {
            const vx: i64 = @as(i64, hull[v].x) - @as(i64, a.x);
            const vy: i64 = @as(i64, hull[v].y) - @as(i64, a.y);
            const cr: i64 = ex * vy - ey * vx;
            // invariant: a cross product of an in-image edge against an in-image vector stays far under the i64 ceiling.
            assert(cr < (@as(i64, 1) << 40));
            assert(cr > -(@as(i64, 1) << 40));
            if (v == 0) {
                min_cross = cr;
                max_cross = cr;
            } else {
                if (cr < min_cross) min_cross = cr;
                if (cr > max_cross) max_cross = cr;
            }
        }

        const cross_range: i64 = max_cross - min_cross;
        // invariant: a range is a difference of a max and a min, never negative.
        assert(cross_range >= 0);
        const num: u64 = @as(u64, @intCast(cross_range)) * @as(u64, @intCast(cross_range));
        const den: u64 = edge_len_sq(a, b);
        const edge_len: f64 = @sqrt(@as(f64, @floatFromInt(den)));
        // invariant: distinct supporting-edge vertices give a positive length, a safe divisor.
        assert(edge_len > 0.0);
        const ang: f64 = std.math.atan2(@as(f64, @floatFromInt(ey)), @as(f64, @floatFromInt(ex)));

        samples[e] = WidthSample{
            .support_i = @intCast(e),
            .support_j = @intCast((e + 1) % hull.len),
            .width_sq_num = num,
            .width_sq_den = den,
            .width = @as(f64, @floatFromInt(cross_range)) / edge_len,
            .angle = ang,
        };

        if (width_less(num, den, samples[min_index].width_sq_num, samples[min_index].width_sq_den)) min_index = e;
        if (width_greater(num, den, samples[max_index].width_sq_num, samples[max_index].width_sq_den)) max_index = e;
    }

    // invariant: both extremes seat a real sample inside the profile.
    assert(min_index < samples.len);
    assert(max_index < samples.len);
    // invariant: the maximum edge-normal width is never below the minimum -- the two extremes bound the profile.
    assert(!width_less(samples[max_index].width_sq_num, samples[max_index].width_sq_den, samples[min_index].width_sq_num, samples[min_index].width_sq_den));

    return WidthProfile{ .samples = samples, .min_index = @intCast(min_index), .max_index = @intCast(max_index) };
}

/// The narrowest sample -- the shape's BREADTH, byte-for-byte image/min_width.rye's result.
pub fn min_sample(profile: WidthProfile) WidthSample {
    // invariant: a real profile carries at least one sample and a valid minimum index.
    assert(profile.samples.len >= 1);
    assert(profile.min_index < profile.samples.len);
    return profile.samples[@as(usize, profile.min_index)];
}

/// The widest edge-normal sample -- the maximum over the hull's own edge orientations, honestly distinct from the true diameter.
pub fn max_sample(profile: WidthProfile) WidthSample {
    // invariant: a real profile carries at least one sample and a valid maximum index.
    assert(profile.samples.len >= 1);
    assert(profile.max_index < profile.samples.len);
    return profile.samples[@as(usize, profile.max_index)];
}

/// The ANISOTROPY -- the widest edge-normal width over the breadth, a dimensionless elongation in [1, inf). A square reads 1
/// (isotropic); a 2x8 bar reads 4. Read in f64 because it divides a square root by a square root; the extremes were selected by
/// exact integer arithmetic.
pub fn anisotropy(profile: WidthProfile) f64 {
    const lo = min_sample(profile);
    const hi = max_sample(profile);
    // invariant: the breadth of a real shape is strictly positive, a safe divisor.
    assert(lo.width > 0.0);
    // invariant: the widest width is never below the breadth, so the ratio is at least one.
    assert(hi.width >= lo.width);
    return hi.width / lo.width;
}

/// TRUE when the shape is as wide as it is thin in every edge direction -- its widest edge-normal width equals its breadth EXACTLY,
/// decided by `max_num * min_den == min_num * max_den`, never a float compare. A square is isotropic; any non-square rectangle is not.
pub fn is_isotropic(profile: WidthProfile) bool {
    const lo = min_sample(profile);
    const hi = max_sample(profile);
    // invariant: both denominators are positive squared edge lengths.
    assert(lo.width_sq_den >= 1);
    assert(hi.width_sq_den >= 1);
    const lhs: i128 = @as(i128, hi.width_sq_num) * @as(i128, lo.width_sq_den);
    const rhs: i128 = @as(i128, lo.width_sq_num) * @as(i128, hi.width_sq_den);
    return lhs == rhs;
}

/// TRUE when the widest edge-normal width is at least `ratio` times the breadth -- the shape is at least that elongated, decided by
/// the exact rule `max_num * min_den >= ratio^2 * min_num * max_den` (the square root cleared by squaring), never a float compare.
/// The honest anisotropy dual of image/min_width.rye's `passes_slot`.
pub fn at_least_as_elongated(profile: WidthProfile, ratio: u32) bool {
    const lo = min_sample(profile);
    const hi = max_sample(profile);
    // invariant: both denominators are positive squared edge lengths.
    assert(lo.width_sq_den >= 1);
    assert(hi.width_sq_den >= 1);
    // invariant: the ratio stays in the coordinate range, so ratio^2 * min_num * max_den stays far under the i128 ceiling.
    assert(ratio <= width_profile_max_ratio);
    const lhs: i128 = @as(i128, hi.width_sq_num) * @as(i128, lo.width_sq_den);
    const rhs: i128 = @as(i128, ratio) * @as(i128, ratio) * @as(i128, lo.width_sq_num) * @as(i128, hi.width_sq_den);
    return lhs >= rhs;
}

// --- selftest: the profile SEES elongation the breadth is blind to (a 2x2 square anisotropy 1 vs a 2x8 bar anisotropy 4, same min
//     width 2 proven against image/min_width.rye); the minimum sample IS the breadth; the maximum sample is edge-flush NOT the
//     diameter (proven against image/convex_diameter.rye); is_isotropic and at_least_as_elongated both ways; refusals; determinism --

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Paint a solid `bw` x `bh` foreground block with its top-left corner at (x, y).
fn set_block(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32, bw: u32, bh: u32) void {
    // invariant: the block stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x + bw <= img.width);
    assert(y + bh <= img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y;
    while (yy < y + bh) : (yy += 1) {
        var xx: u32 = x;
        while (xx < x + bw) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yy);
    }
}

fn approx(a: f64, b: f64, tol: f64) bool {
    const d = if (a >= b) a - b else b - a;
    return d <= tol;
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- THE PROFILE SEES ELONGATION THE BREADTH IS BLIND TO -- a 2x2 square and a 2x8 bar share a minimum width of EXACTLY 2, yet
    //     the square is ISOTROPIC (every edge-normal width 2, anisotropy 1) while the bar runs 2 and 8 (anisotropy EXACTLY 4) -----
    var square_pts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 2, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 2, .y = 2 },
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 2 },
    };
    const square = try width_profile(garden, square_pts[0..]);
    if (!approx(min_sample(square).width, 2.0, 1e-9)) return error.SquareBreadthNotTwo;
    if (!approx(max_sample(square).width, 2.0, 1e-9)) return error.SquareMaxNotTwo;
    if (!is_isotropic(square)) return error.SquareNotIsotropic;
    if (!approx(anisotropy(square), 1.0, 1e-9)) return error.SquareAnisotropyNotOne;

    var bar_pts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 2, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 2, .y = 8 },
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 8 },
    };
    const bar = try width_profile(garden, bar_pts[0..]);
    if (!approx(min_sample(bar).width, 2.0, 1e-9)) return error.BarBreadthNotTwo;
    if (!approx(max_sample(bar).width, 8.0, 1e-9)) return error.BarMaxNotEight;
    if (is_isotropic(bar)) return error.BarFalselyIsotropic;
    if (!approx(anisotropy(bar), 4.0, 1e-9)) return error.BarAnisotropyNotFour;
    // the square and the bar share one breadth the profile separates: exact squared widths 4/1 == 4/1
    if (min_sample(square).width_sq_num * min_sample(bar).width_sq_den != min_sample(bar).width_sq_num * min_sample(square).width_sq_den)
        return error.SharedBreadthDiffers;

    // --- THE MINIMUM SAMPLE IS THE BREADTH -- the profile's floor is byte-for-byte image/min_width.rye's result (same exact rational)
    const bar_breadth = try min_width.min_width(garden, bar_pts[0..]);
    if (min_sample(bar).width_sq_num != bar_breadth.width_sq_num or min_sample(bar).width_sq_den != bar_breadth.width_sq_den)
        return error.ProfileFloorDiffersFromMinWidth;

    // --- THE MAXIMUM SAMPLE IS EDGE-FLUSH, NOT THE DIAMETER -- for the bar the widest edge-normal width is EXACTLY 8, STRICTLY below
    //     the true diameter sqrt(68) ~ 8.25 (image/convex_diameter.rye), whose antipodal span is not edge-aligned ---------------
    const bar_hull = try convex_hull.convex_hull(garden, bar_pts[0..]);
    const bar_diam = try convex_diameter.convex_diameter(bar_hull);
    if (!approx(bar_diam.dist, @sqrt(68.0), 1e-6)) return error.BarDiameterWrong;
    if (max_sample(bar).width >= bar_diam.dist) return error.MaxNotBelowDiameter; // 8 << 8.25, the edge-flush-versus-antipodal gap

    // --- is_isotropic BOTH WAYS -- a 6x2 rectangle is anisotropic (3x), never isotropic -----------------------------------
    var rect_pts = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 6, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 6, .y = 2 },
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 2 },
    };
    const rect = try width_profile(garden, rect_pts[0..]);
    if (is_isotropic(rect)) return error.RectFalselyIsotropic;
    if (!approx(anisotropy(rect), 3.0, 1e-9)) return error.RectAnisotropyNotThree;

    // --- at_least_as_elongated -- the bar is at least 4x elongated, refuses 5x; the square is not even 2x (it is 1x) --------
    if (!at_least_as_elongated(bar, 4)) return error.BarNotFourElongated;
    if (at_least_as_elongated(bar, 5)) return error.BarFalselyFiveElongated;
    if (at_least_as_elongated(square, 2)) return error.SquareFalselyTwoElongated;
    if (!at_least_as_elongated(square, 1)) return error.SquareNotOneElongated; // every shape is at least 1x elongated

    // --- AN INTEGRATED RASTER CRUX -- a solid 5x5 block traced through image/label.rye and image/contour.rye is ISOTROPIC (every
    //     edge-normal width EXACTLY 4, the 1..5 corner extent), anisotropy 1 ----------------------------------------------
    var big = try make_canvas(garden, 7, 7, 0);
    set_block(&big, 1, 1, 5, 5);
    const bmap = try label.label_components(garden, &big);
    const bring = try contour.trace_contour(garden, &bmap, 1);
    const block = try width_profile(garden, bring.points);
    if (!approx(min_sample(block).width, 4.0, 1e-9)) return error.BlockBreadthNotFour;
    if (!is_isotropic(block)) return error.BlockNotIsotropic;
    if (!approx(anisotropy(block), 1.0, 1e-9)) return error.BlockAnisotropyNotOne;

    // --- DEGENERATE hulls refuse DegenerateHull: a single point, and three COLLINEAR points (whose hull is a two-point segment)
    //     have no width to measure ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var one = [_]contour.Point{.{ .x = 4, .y = 7 }};
    if (width_profile(garden, one[0..])) |_| return error.OnePointNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.DegenerateHull) return error.WrongOnePointError;
    }
    var colinear = [_]contour.Point{
        .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 3, .y = 0 },
        .{ .x = 6, .y = 0 },
    };
    if (width_profile(garden, colinear[0..])) |_| return error.CollinearNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.DegenerateHull) return error.WrongCollinearError;
    }

    // --- REFUSALS through the hull: an empty input refuses EmptySource, having allocated nothing --------------------------
    const empty: []const contour.Point = &[_]contour.Point{};
    if (width_profile(garden, empty)) |_| return error.EmptyNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.EmptySource) return error.WrongEmptyError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM: the same point set reads the same profile twice --------------------------------------------------
    const p1 = try width_profile(garden, bar_pts[0..]);
    const p2 = try width_profile(garden, bar_pts[0..]);
    if (p1.min_index != p2.min_index or p1.max_index != p2.max_index or p1.samples.len != p2.samples.len)
        return error.ProfileNotDeterministic;

    print("GREEN image-width-profile: the WIDTH PROFILE of a point set -- the full per-orientation width(theta) anisotropy signature, one perpendicular-extent sample flush with EVERY image/convex_hull.rye edge, the minimum of which is exactly image/min_width.rye's breadth, min and max selected by the SAME EXACT i128 comparison and NO tolerance -- the DECISIVE crux: a 2x2 square and a 2x8 bar share a minimum width of EXACTLY 2 (proven against image/min_width.rye), yet the square's every edge-normal width is 2 (ISOTROPIC, anisotropy 1) while the bar's run 2 and 8 (anisotropy EXACTLY 4), so the profile SEES the elongation the single breadth number is blind to; the minimum sample IS the breadth byte-for-byte; the maximum sample is EDGE-FLUSH not the diameter -- for the bar the widest edge-normal width is EXACTLY 8, STRICTLY below the true diameter sqrt(68) ~ 8.25 (image/convex_diameter.rye), whose antipodal span is not edge-aligned; is_isotropic decides a square true and a 6x2 rectangle false (anisotropy 3) by exact equality; at_least_as_elongated reads the bar 4x not 5x and the square 1x not 2x by exact integer arithmetic; a solid 5x5 raster block traced through label and contour is isotropic; a single point and three collinear points refuse DegenerateHull, an empty input EmptySource, each having allocated nothing; the same point set reads deterministically\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: width_profile selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
